\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Signed zeros cancel}
\date{2026-11-23}

\p{Let #{f:R\to k^n} on the \vocabk{simplicial momentum grid}{fdip-013X}
over an ordered field. At each \vocabk{regular value}{fdip-013Y} #{y},
#{\deg(f;y)=0}. When every vertex zero is nondegenerate, the
local and off-grid degrees are well defined and
##{\sum_{f(r)=0}\deg_r f+\deg^\circ(f)=0.}
In particular the vertex degrees sum to zero if no simplex
without a zero vertex contains a zero of #{\tilde f}.}

\proof{
\p{Give each ordered simplex #{[v_0,\ldots,v_n]} the coefficient
#{\operatorname{sgn}(\pi)}. In its boundary the face omitting
#{v_i} has the further factor #{(-1)^i}. For #{0<i<n} the same
ordered face occurs with opposite coefficient upon interchanging
steps #{i} and #{i+1}. The face omitting #{v_0} occurs again
as the last omission in the cube starting at #{v_1}, with steps
#{\pi(2),\ldots,\pi(n),\pi(1)}. The new factor is
#{(-1)^n(-1)^{n-1}=-1}, also across a periodic seam.
Thus the oriented sum has zero boundary.}

\p{For any #{n+2} ordered image points #{b_i\in k^n}, consider
their alternating boundary of #{n}-simplices. Suppose #{y} is
in no hull of at most #{n} of these points. Its signed count on
this boundary is zero. Indeed, let #{B} have columns #{(1,b_i)}
and set #{c_i=(-1)^i\det B_{\widehat i}}, deleting column #{i}.
Subtracting the first retained column from the others shows that
#{\operatorname{sgn} c_i} is precisely the signed orientation
of that boundary face. If #{B} lacks full row rank all counts
vanish. Otherwise cofactor expansion gives #{Bc=0}, and the
solutions of #{B\lambda=(1,y)} form a line with direction #{c}.}

\p{The conditions #{\lambda_i\ge0} cut this line in an empty
set or a bounded closed interval, since #{c\ne0} and
#{\sum_i c_i=0}. Its endpoints are finite maxima and minima
of coordinate bounds. Two zero coordinates at once would place
#{y} in a forbidden hull. Thus a nonempty interval has distinct
endpoints, as a singleton would attain both a lower and an upper
bound. No coordinate can vanish identically there, since another
vanishes at an endpoint. Exactly two facets then contain #{y},
with #{c_i>0} at the entering end and #{c_j<0} at the leaving
end. Their contributions cancel.}

\p{Cone each image simplex #{[a_0,\ldots,a_n]} to one point #{q},
prepending #{q} to its vertex list. Choose #{q\ne y} as follows.
For each nonempty face of at most #{n-1} original vertices with
image affine span #{H}, avoid #{\operatorname{aff}(H,y)} if
#{y\notin H}, and avoid #{H} otherwise. These are finitely many
proper affine subspaces. Each lies in an affine hyperplane. The product of the defining
affine polynomials is nonzero and cannot vanish on all of #{k^n},
since an ordered field is infinite. Then #{y} lies
in no augmented hull of at most #{n} vertices. When #{y\in H},
its weight on #{q} would have to be zero and original regularity
excludes the remaining hull. The cone boundary consists of the
base minus the cone on its boundary. Applying the preceding
identity and canceling all paired side faces leaves
#{\deg(f;y)=0}, including for collapsed image simplices.}

\p{Fix a nondegenerate vertex zero #{r}. In the sum of incident
simplices the faces containing #{r} cancel in pairs. The remaining
boundary faces opposite #{r} have linearly independent vertex
values. Their affine spans omit zero. This time choose #{q\ne0}
outside those spans and outside #{\operatorname{aff}(0,F)} for
each nonempty proper subface image #{F} of these opposite faces.
Every cone on an opposite face is nondegenerate, and none of
its facet hyperplanes contains zero. Its signed count is constant
in some box about zero because its affine barycentric coordinates
retain their strict signs there. A common box works for the
finitely many cones.}

\p{For a regular probe #{y} in that box, the signed count in the
star of #{r} equals this coned boundary count. To apply the cone
identity, one may first move #{y} slightly inside the box to
avoid every affine span of a face of at most #{n} vertices in the
augmented simplices. Every original star simplex contains #{y}
with all barycentric coordinates positive or misses it with at
least one negative, so its count persists under this move.
Every open box contains a point outside finitely many proper
affine subspaces by the same polynomial argument. This also
works for a probe regular only in the star. For #{y=tu} with
#{u} as in the definition of local degree, its coefficients on
the #{n} other vertex values are #{t} times the coordinates of
#{u} in their basis. For small #{t>0} the remaining coefficient
is positive. The star count is therefore #{\deg_r f}, independent
of this direction.}

\p{Finally choose a direction for the [off-grid degree](fdip-013Y). Spans missing zero meet its ray at most once,
so #{tu} is regular for all sufficiently small #{t>0}.
Two distinct nondegenerate zero vertices cannot share a simplex,
since one of the other's independent values would be zero.
Subtracting their star counts from the zero global count gives
the stated choice-independent off-grid degree. If a remaining
simplex misses zero, its contribution vanishes near zero.
An invertible image simplex has a negative barycentric coordinate
at zero, and a singular one has orientation zero. Finiteness gives
one bound for them all.}
}

\p{An image simplex of zero determinant cannot contain a regular
value. In a positive barycentric representation with affinely
dependent support, subtract a multiple of an affine dependence
until the first positive weight becomes zero. This removes a
vertex without changing the point. Mathlib's
\leanref{Caratheodory.mem_convexHull_erase} gives this deletion
for an affinely dependent finite set.}
